{"id":"4a7dcfb4-8da8-4b77-86a8-cf1ed5fbcc08","object":"chat.completion","created":1784823313,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload', case)\\n    required = ('initial_state', 'max_steps', 'tool_observations', 'transitions')\\n    for k in required:\\n        if k not in payload:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing field: {k}')\\n    initial = payload['initial_state']\\n    max_steps = payload['max_steps']\\n    tool_obs = payload['tool_observations']\\n    transitions = payload['transitions']\\n    if not isinstance(max_steps, int) or max_steps < 0:\\n        raise ValueError('max_steps must be non-negative integer')\\n    if initial not in transitions:\\n        raise ValueError(f'Initial state {initial!r} not in transitions')\\n    # Validate all transitions\\n    for st, defn in transitions.items():\\n        if 'kind' not in defn:\\n            raise ValueError(f'State {st!r} missing kind')\\n        kind = defn['kind']\\n        if kind == 'final':\\n            if 'value' not in defn:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Final state {st!r} missing value')\\n        elif kind == 'action':\\n            if 'action' not in defn:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Action state {st!r} missing action')\\n            if 'next_by_observation' not in defn:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Action state {st!r} missing next_by_observation')\\n            if 'default_next' not in defn:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Action state {st!r} missing default_next')\\n            action = defn['action']\\n            if action not in tool_obs:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Tool {action!r} not in tool_observations')\\n            nxt_by_obs = defn['next_by_observation']\\n            if not isinstance(nxt_by_obs, dict):\\n                raise ValueError(f'next_by_observation must be dict')\\n            for obs, nxt in nxt_by_obs.items():\\n                if nxt not in transitions:\\n                    raise ValueError(f'State {nxt!r} referenced from {st!r} not in transitions')\\n            def_nxt = defn['default_next']\\n            if def_nxt not in transitions:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Default next {def_nxt!r} from {st!r} not in transitions')\\n        else:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Invalid kind {kind!r} in state {st!r}')\\n    # Simulation\\n    state = initial\\n    trace = []\\n    step_count = 0\\n    while True:\\n        defn = transitions[state]\\n        if defn['kind'] == 'final':\\n            trace.append({'state': state, 'kind': 'final', 'value': defn['value']})\\n            return {'status': 'completed', 'final_value': defn['value'], 'trace': trace}\\n        elif defn['kind'] == 'action':\\n            if step_count >= max_steps:\\n                break\\n            action = defn['action']\\n            observation = tool_obs[action]\\n            nxt_by_obs = defn['next_by_observation']\\n            if observation in nxt_by_obs:\\n                next_state = nxt_by_obs[observation]\\n            else:\\n                next_state = defn['default_next']\\n            trace.append({'state': state, 'action': action, 'observation': observation, 'next_state': next_state})\\n            state = next_state\\n            step_count += 1\\n        else:\\n            raise ValueError('Unexpected kind')\\n    # max steps reached without final\\n    return {'status': 'max_steps_exceeded', 'final_value': None, 'trace': trace}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements a ReAct tool loop controller as described. The case payload has fields: initial_state, max_steps, tool_observations, transitions. Transitions are a dict mapping state names to state definitions. Each state definition has kind ('action' or 'final'), action (for action states), next_by_observation (dict mapping observation string to next state), default_next (string). For final states, value is provided.\n\nThe function should simulate the loop: start at initial_state, for up to max_steps steps, execute actions, record observations, follow transitions. If max_steps reached without final state, return appropriate status (e.g., \"max_steps_exceeded\"? The spec says \"Stop on completion or max steps and return status, final value, and trace.\" So we need to decide return structure.\n\nFrom the description: \"return status, final value, and trace.\" Likely status is \"completed\" or \"max_steps_exceeded\", final value from final state or None, and trace is the sequence of states, actions, observations.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use only Python standard library. Return JSON-serializable object. Raise ValueError for invalid case (e.g., missing fields, invalid transitions, etc.)\n\nWe'll implement `solve(case)` that extracts payload, validates, then simulates.\n\nDefine the trace as list of steps: each step could be a dict with state, action (if any), observation (if any), next_state.\n\nReturn dict: { \"status\": ..., \"final_value\": ..., \"trace\": ... }.\n\nEdge cases: tool_observations dict provides mapping from tool name to observation string. It's fixed per tool, not changing. For each action state, we need to check if tool is in tool_observations? Actually tool_observations is given as a dict: e.g., \"lookup\": \"ok\". So when we dispatch tool, we just get that observation. Then we look up in next_by_observation for that observation; if not found, use default_next.\n\nIf default_next is missing? Assume it's always present? The schema shows default_next always present. But we should handle missing fields.\n\nIf action state has no action field? invalid.\n\nIf next state doesn't exist in transitions? treat as invalid? Or handle gracefully? The spec says raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. So we should validate before simulation.\n\nAlso, if max_steps is reached and not in final state, we should stop and return status \"max_steps_exceeded\" with final_value None? Or maybe we should still return the last state? Likely return the trace up to that point, and status accordingly.\n\nWe also need to handle the case where a transition is missing both observation and default? But schema provides default.\n\nNow produce Python source as a single string within JSON.\n\nWe'll write the function with comprehensive validation.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Check payload has required keys.\n- Extract initial_state, max_steps, tool_observations, transitions.\n- Validate: max_steps >= 0? integer? Assume positive.\n- Validate all states in transitions exist for next_by_observation values and default_next.\n- Validate each transition state has kind in {\"action\",\"final\"}.\n- If action, has action and action is a string; has next_by_observation (dict) and default_next (string).\n- If final, has value.\n- Optionally validate tool_observations keys match actions used? Not strictly required but could be.\n- Simulate:\n    state = initial_state\n    step_count = 0\n    trace = []\n    while step_count < max_steps:\n        if state not in transitions: raise ValueError\n        defn = transitions[state]\n        if defn['kind'] == 'final':\n            # termination\n            trace.append({'state': state, 'kind': 'final', 'value': defn['value']})\n            return {'status': 'completed', 'final_value': defn['value'], 'trace': trace}\n        elif defn['kind'] == 'action':\n            action = defn['action']\n            if action not in tool_observations: raise ValueError(\"Unknown tool\")\n            observation = tool_observations[action]\n            # look up next state\n            if observation in defn['next_by_observation']:\n                next_state = defn['next_by_observation'][observation]\n            else:\n                next_state = defn['default_next']\n            trace.append({'state': state, 'action': action, 'observation': observation, 'next_state': next_state})\n            state = next_state\n            step_count += 1\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid kind\")\n    # max steps reached\n    # after loop, if state is final? But we exited loop because step_count == max_steps, so we didn't process final state? Actually the loop condition is step_count < max_steps; if we are at final state, we return. So if we exit loop, we have not hit final state. So current state might be action or final? But we never processed final? Actually if we get to final state, we return immediately, so loop ends only if max_steps reached before final. So state is the last state after last action (could be final? No, because if state is final we would have returned. So state is non-final action state or maybe bad state? But bad is final as per example. Actually \"bad\" is final. So if we reach bad, we would have returned earlier? Let's see: If we take default_next to 'bad', that is a final state. But we only check for final at the beginning of loop, so we will process 'bad' as final and return. So after loop, state is not final because we would have returned. If state is final but max_steps reached exactly at step_count = max_steps? The loop condition step_count < max_steps, if step_count == max_steps, loop exits before checking final. But step_count increments after action, so if after action we go to a final state and step_count is now max_steps, we exit loop before checking final. That's a problem: we need to check final state even after last action if step_count reached max_steps. The spec says \"Stop on completion or max steps\". Completion means reaching a final state. So if we reach final state exactly at max_steps, we should treat as completion, not max_steps_exceeded. So we should restructure: after each transition, if new state is final, we should record and return even if step_count == max_steps. Alternatively, do the check at the beginning of loop, but we need to consider that after last action we might be in final. Better: after updating state and step_count, check if state is final. If yes, return completion. Then continue loop only if step_count < max_steps. So code:\n\nwhile True:\n    if state not in transitions: raise...\n    defn = transitions[state]\n    if defn['kind'] == 'final':\n        trace.append(...)\n        return ... completed\n    elif defn['kind'] == 'action':\n        if step_count >= max_steps: # no more actions allowed\n            # we have reached max steps without final; break out\n            break\n        action = ...\n        # get observation, next_state\n        # record step\n        state = next_state\n        step_count += 1\n        # after this, continue loop (will check final at top)\n    else: raise\n# after loop (max steps reached without final)\nreturn status 'max_steps_exceeded', final_value None, trace (which includes all steps up to last action)\n\nBut we need to handle case where initial state is final: then we return immediately with 0 steps.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nWe need to ensure the function uses only standard library, no imports besides maybe json? Not needed for function itself. We can just return dict.\n\nWrite `solve(case)` with `case` being a dict (as parsed from JSON). It should return a dict.\n\nNow write the Python source string.\n\nWe'll escape properly for JSON: double quotes, backslashes, newlines as \\n.\n\nOutput as JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string.\n\nLet's produce."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1265,"completion_tokens":2607,"total_tokens":3872,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1736},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":113},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}